  "body": "## Problem\n\n`detect_holes` on a Drill operation takes holes that are larger than the drill. `generate` and `verify` then pass with no warning, and `post` writes the program. Each such hole is drilled at the drill diameter, so the finished hole is too small. Nothing tells the user.\n\nThis is the Drill twin of #1556 (Bore), which is closed.\n\n## Reproduction (main at 983f527bd, `camcraft-cli mcp`)\n\nPart: a plate 120 x 80 x 10 mm with a boss, exported from SolidCraft as STEP. Through holes: two of D6, two of D8, two of D12.\n\n```\nimport_model   { \"path\": \"platte_zapfen.step\", \"name\": \"Platte\" }\nfit_stock      { \"sides\": [2, 2, 2, 2, 0, 0] }\nadd_tool       { \"catalog\": \"DR-0600\" }\nadd_operation  { \"kind\": \"drill\", \"tool\": \"DR-0600\", \"source\": \"Platte\", \"name\": \"Bohren\" }\ndetect_holes   { \"name\": \"Bohren\" }        -> \"holes\": 6, \"skipped\": []\ngenerate       { \"name\": \"Bohren\" }        -> status ok, warnings []\n```\n\nThe operation holds holes of radius 3, 4 and 6 (D6, D8, D12). The cache has six `deep` cycles and no warning.\n\n## Expected result\n\nOne of these, by name:\n\n- `detect_holes` skips a hole whose diameter is not the drill diameter (within a tolerance), and lists it in `skipped` with the reason (for example \"D8.000 hole, D6.000 drill\"), or\n- `generate` refuses the operation by name, or\n- the operation gets a warning that names each undersize hole (for a pilot drill, a user can accept it).\n\n## Proposed priority\n\n`P3` by the AGENTS.md table: a wrong part size with no message, and a workaround (pick the holes by hand). The tool path itself does not gouge or collide.\n",
